  "body": "The [K492 review](docs/research/community-k492-review.md) leaves one obligation open for #621 (Ali Romman). That PR's `L`-uniform inverse bound still needs paid costs for efficient preparation, rounded execution, restoration and tape. #628 (Chafik Boukhalfa) pays for preparation and neighbouring-stride execution. #764 (Charles Bethin) sharpens the scan rounding. For any stride other than \u00b11, #628 routes cycles through two merge sorts costing `O(L(B+log L) log L)`. Those sorts bring back the `log L` factor that the inverse bound removed.\n\nThis PR adds `research/cyclic-inverse-stride-bridge/` and one CI workflow. **No existing file changes. No new integer \u03ba, complex b, or BIT c.**\n\n## Result\n\n- **Theorem 1.** A Euclidean chain/closure recursion solves `y_i = x_i + b_i y_(i-w)` for any stride. A lag-`w` FIFO built from two stacks gives every pass its predecessor in natural order. Each level at least halves the length. A single stable factor then costs:\n  - at most `40L` sequential record moves (largest observed: 29.68 per entry through `L=131072`);\n  - `O(L)` rounded operations;\n  - an error of at most `min(K,1/\u03b2)(u/\u03b2)(5+4B/\u03b2)+u`.\n\n  The error bound holds for every `L`. The proof reuses #764's shared-suffix identity `1-Q \u2265 \u03b2G`. A concavity argument shows that coefficient margins never fall below `\u03b2` at any recursion depth.\n- **Theorem 2.** Composing the solver with #628's certified factorization gives `P(\u03c4Z_L^w)^{-1}` at precision `p+(n+2)g+O(log r) = p+O(r^4)`, with no `L` term. One admission condition, `p \u2265 log2(log2 L+2)`, depends on `L`.\n- **Verdict.** For one kernel inverse on fixed sequential tapes, the cost with all four obligations paid is `soft-O(r^3(p+r^4)) + O(rL)\u00b7M(p+r^4) + O(rL(p+r^4))`. That cost per entry does not depend on `L`, so **#621's gain survives payment at the single-kernel level**. Restoration in this model is cheap: the input tape is never written, and scratch tapes are erased at linear cost.\n\n## Validation\n\n`python3 -B -I -O research/cyclic-inverse-stride-bridge/verify.py --check` prints `PASS_CYCLIC_INVERSE_STRIDE_BRIDGE generic=2052 kernel_cases=300 controls=4 max_records_per_entry=29.671875`. It passed under system Python and the repository venv (Python 3.13, Linux). It needs only the standard library and takes about 100 seconds.\n\n- **Generic cases (2052).** `L=1..18`, every stride in `[-L,L)`, three margins and two grids. Each case is compared exactly with an independent per-cycle geometric-closure oracle. The margin invariant is checked at every recursion level.\n- **Kernel cases (300).** These use all ten #628 certified kernels (radix 3 through 16), two gauges, `L \u2208 {1,2,3,5,8}` and all strides for `L\u22645`. Each case checks Theorem 2's exact budget together with #628's perturbation bound and the expanded residual. 81 cases are also compared with a dense Gauss\u2013Jordan solve.\n- **Controls.** The replay rejects a rotated closure reseed, too little precision, a unit coefficient and a wrong inner-shift direction.\n- **Vendored sources.** The #628 files are byte-identical to head `0768f97d` and are hash-pinned; the verifier refuses drift.\n- **Repository checks (Linux, base `66849e1`).** I did not complete a full `make verify`.\n  - `make verify` passed seven targets with no failures: `verify-community`, `verify-producers`, `verify-partial-gauge`, `verify-three-stage-cover`, `verify-paired-cube`, `verify-recycled-bit` and `verify-entrance-banks`. My one-hour local limit then stopped the run during `gen4-bank-verify`.\n  - `make selected-record-check` and `make verify-tests` passed separately: 549 tests, 0 failures, exit 0.\n  - I did not run `frontier626-verify` or the later targets. This PR changes no existing file and does not affect the selected construction.\n  - `git diff --cached --check` passed.\n\n## Still open\n\n- A borrowed-array (dirty) form of the stride solver for the exact-width model. #628's dirty route still covers that model, at `O(p+log(D+1)+r^5)` precision.\n- Composition across the complete transform, phase collection and normalization.\n- The multiplication reduction.\n- The `O(r^4)` guard is a proof bound. Numerical probes put the true `min|P|` near `2^-10` for `r\u226432`; those probe values are not certified here.\n\nThis is not a formal verification. The written proof carries the all-`L` claims, and the finite cases support the reference implementation.\n\n## Credits\n\n- **#621, Ali Romman (@aliromman).** Kernel and the `L`-uniform bounds.\n- **#628, Chafik Boukhalfa (@chafreaky).** Factorization, preparation, phase and dirty-shear budgets, and the vendored certificates.\n- **#764, Charles Bethin (@cbethin).** The shared-suffix identity and the inverse-square scan bound.\n\nNew work by **Rohan Guliani (@Rohansguliani)**, prepared with substantial AI assistance. Apache-2.0. No literature-priority claim is made.",
